# Project registry for the destructive scripts (remove-protection.sh, restore-protection.sh,
# destroy.sh). Copy this file to hcloud-projects.conf — which is gitignored and must never
# be committed — or, better, let the scripts write it for you:
#
#   ./remove-protection.sh --project test --register
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# A Hetzner Cloud API token carries no project name, so a script cannot ask which project
# it is pointed at. Without that, `--project test` would be a comment rather than a check.
# Each line binds a project name to the SHA-256 fingerprint (first 12 hex characters) of
# the token that belongs to it. The scripts fingerprint the token they loaded and abort if
# it does not match the project named on the command line.
#
# The fingerprint is a one-way hash: this file cannot be used to recover a token, and it is
# safe to print in logs. It is gitignored anyway, on the principle that a file mapping your
# project names to your credentials is nobody else's business.
#
# Format:  [!]<project-name>:<sha256-of-token, first 12 hex chars>
#
# A leading '!' marks the project PROTECTED. Scripts that destroy or weaken resources
# (remove-protection.sh, destroy.sh) refuse a protected project outright — there is no
# flag, no --force and no --apply that overrides it. Scripts that only move in the safe
# direction (restore-protection.sh) keep working.
#
# Mark a project protected:
#   ./remove-protection.sh --project prod --protect
#
# Un-marking it is deliberately NOT a flag: edit this file by hand and delete the '!'.
# A safety rail you can remove with a flag is a safety rail you remove by accident, and
# the hand edit is what makes the intent explicit.
#
# Example (fingerprints below are made up):
#
# !prod:1a2b3c4d5e6f      <- production: destructive scripts refuse this outright
# test:0f9e8d7c6b5a       <- throwaway project used for the green-field proof
